UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'Frida Kahlo was eighteen years old when a streetcar collided with the bus she was riding in Mexico City. A metal handrail impaled her abdomen and exited through her pelvis. Her spine was broken in three places. Her collarbone was broken. Her ribs were broken. Her pelvis was shattered. Her right leg was broken in eleven places. Her right foot was crushed. Doctors did not expect her to survive.

But she did survive—only to face a lifetime of chronic, excruciating pain. Over her life, she endured more than thirty surgeries. She wore medical corsets. She spent months in body casts. She could barely walk for long periods. Some days, the pain was so intense she could not get out of bed. Her dreams of becoming a doctor were shattered. The body that had once been strong and athletic was now a source of constant agony and limitation.

During her long recovery, unable to move, Frida began to paint—initially out of boredom, later out of necessity. A special easel was constructed so she could paint while lying down. A mirror was installed above her bed so she could paint self-portraits. And what she painted was not escapist fantasy or pretty landscapes to deny her reality. She painted her pain. She painted her broken body, her suffering, her rage, her despair—but also her defiance, her sensuality, her fierce love of life.

Her paintings are raw, honest, sometimes shocking. She painted herself in a body cast, nails driven into her flesh. She painted her spine as a crumbling column. She painted her miscarriages and her inability to have children. She painted the physical and emotional pain that was her constant companion. Through art, she transformed suffering into beauty, wounds into creativity, limitation into expression.

She said: "I paint myself because I am so often alone and because I am the subject I know best." Her art was not therapy in the soothing sense—it was confrontation with reality, honest witness to pain, refusal to look away from what hurt. She did not paint to escape her suffering but to give it form, to make meaning from it, to say: "This is what happened to me. This is what I feel. I will not disappear into suffering—I will transform it into art."

Today, Frida is recognized as one of Mexico''s greatest artists. Her story teaches that we can transform suffering into beauty through creative expression. Pain can become our art if we have the courage to look at it honestly, to give it form, to share it with the world. Our wounds do not disqualify us from creating beauty—sometimes they are the very source of it.'
WHERE title = 'Frida Kahlo''s Art';

UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'Helen Keller lost both her sight and hearing to illness at nineteen months old. Before she could form memories, before she could learn to speak or read, she was plunged into a world of permanent darkness and silence. She could not communicate with anyone. She had no language. She lived in what she later described as a "dense fog" of isolation, trapped inside her own mind with no way to connect with the world around her.

She became a wild, frustrated child, throwing violent tantrums because she had no way to express her needs, wants, or feelings. Her family did not know how to reach her. Many people suggested she be institutionalized. The assumption was that a deaf-blind person could never truly learn, never fully participate in society, never live a meaningful life. She seemed destined for permanent isolation.

But everything changed when Anne Sullivan arrived as her teacher when Helen was six years old. Through patient, persistent work, Anne taught Helen to associate finger-spelled words with objects. The breakthrough came at a water pump when Helen finally understood that the sensation on one hand (water) connected to the finger-spelling on her other hand (W-A-T-E-R). She had discovered language! From that moment, an entire world opened to her. She learned voraciously—Braille, writing, even speaking (though it remained difficult her entire life).

Helen went on to graduate from Radcliffe College (now part of Harvard), becoming the first deaf-blind person to earn a Bachelor of Arts degree. She wrote fourteen books and hundreds of articles. She became a powerful activist for disability rights, women''s suffrage, labor rights, and pacifism. She traveled to thirty-nine countries, met presidents and kings, and inspired millions.

Late in her life, she wrote: "The only thing worse than being blind is having sight but no vision." This profound statement captured what she had learned: physical limitations do not limit vision—the ability to see possibility, to imagine better futures, to dream of change. She knew many people with perfect eyesight who could not envision a world where disabled people had equal rights, where women could vote, where workers were treated fairly. They could see, but they had no vision.

Helen''s life teaches that physical limitations do not limit what truly matters: our capacity for learning, our vision for what could be, our ability to contribute meaningfully to the world. Many who can see perfectly lack the vision to truly live. Helen, who lived in permanent darkness and silence, saw more clearly than most.'
WHERE title = 'Helen Keller''s World';